Fixtures of the tests of the NIR reader and writer (tests/nir.scala). NIR is Scala Native's
intermediate representation: the files its compiler plugin writes into a library's jar.

src/          the Scala sources the fixtures were compiled from, each on its own: Shapes.scala;
              Numbers.scala on x86-64, where the JVM's 0.0 / 0.0 is a NaN of other bits than
              Double.NaN's, so that its files hold two NaN entries of the same bytes; and
              Positions.scala with `-P:scalanative:positionRelativizationPaths:<its own path>`, and
              no main class (tests/native-link takes every main class here for a program of its own)
fixtures/     the .nir files, written by the compiler plugin of Scala Native 0.5.12 for Scala 3.8.4:
                scala-cli compile with `//> using platform native` and `//> using nativeVersion 0.5.12`,
                the files taken from the classes directory; `teq nir --rewrite` has to write
                each again as these bytes
expected.txt  what Scala Native's own reader and printer (nir.serialization and nir.Show of its
              tools 0.5.12) print for the fixtures: under `## <file>`, each definition of the file
              in the order of the mangled names. It is their output, never teq's: `teq nir` has
              to print the same bytes
digests.txt   the same for whole jars, as the SHA-256 of that text with the counts of files and
              definitions, which `teq nir --verify` prints
